      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Here’s a concise overview of what water is, how it’s structured, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Chemical Identity\n- **Formula:** **H₂O** – each molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.\n- **Molecular shape:** Bent (≈104.5° angle) due to the two lone pairs of electrons on oxygen, giving water a polar nature (partial negative charge on O, partial positive on H).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Physical States\n| State | Typical Conditions | Key Characteristics |\n|-------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **Solid (Ice)** | ≤ 0 °C (32 °F) at 1 atm | Crystalline lattice, less dense than liquid water (hence ice floats). |\n| **Liquid** | 0 °C – 100 °C (32 °F – 212 °F) at 1 atm | Highly cohesive, high surface tension, excellent solvent. |\n| **Gas (Steam/Vapor)** | ≥ 100 °C (212 °F) at 1 atm | Molecules far apart, low density; can hold large amounts of heat (latent heat of vaporization). |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Unique Physical & Chemical Properties\n| Property | Why It Happens | Significance |\n|----------|----------------|--------------|\n| **Polarity** | Unequal sharing of electrons → dipole moment | Strong hydrogen bonding → high boiling point, surface tension, and solvent power. |\n| **Hydrogen Bonding** | Attraction between the H of one molecule and O of another | Gives water its high specific heat, high heat of vaporization, and cohesion/adhesion (capillary action). |\n| **High Specific Heat (4.18 J·g⁻¹·K⁻¹)** | Energy needed to break many hydrogen bonds | Buffers temperature changes in oceans, lakes, and living organisms. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization (≈2260 J·g⁻¹)** | Energy required to break hydrogen bonds during evaporation | Drives cooling mechanisms (sweating, transpiration). |\n| **Density Anomaly** | Ice forms an open hexagonal lattice, making it less dense | Ice floats, insulating aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Polar nature dissolves ions and many polar molecules | Enables biochemical reactions, nutrient transport, and geological weathering. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Role in the Environment & Life\n- **Hydrologic Cycle:** Evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → repeat. Water moves energy and matter around the planet.\n- **Biological Functions:**  \n  - **Medium for biochemical reactions** (most occur in aqueous solution).  \n  - **Transport:** Blood, sap, and cellular fluids rely on water.  \n  - **Structural:** Provides turgor pressure in plants, maintains cell shape.  \n  - **Thermoregulation:** Sweating, panting, and evaporative cooling keep organisms from overheating.\n- **Geological Impact:** Weathering of rocks, formation of sedimentary layers, and shaping of landforms (rivers, glaciers, coastlines).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Sources & Availability\n- **Freshwater:** ~2.5 % of Earth’s water; most locked in glaciers, ice caps, and groundwater. Only ~0.3 % is readily accessible surface water (lakes, rivers).  \n- **Saltwater:** ~97.5 % of Earth’s water, primarily in oceans; contains ~3.5 % dissolved salts (mainly NaCl).  \n- **Atmospheric Water:** Water vapor and clouds hold a tiny fraction (~0.001 % of total water) but are crucial for weather and climate.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Everyday Examples\n- **Drinking water:** Essential for human health; provides hydration and electrolytes.  \n- **Cooking:** Solvent for heat transfer and flavor extraction.  \n- **Cleaning:** Dissolves soils and greases.  \n- **Industrial uses:** Coolants, solvents, steam power generation, chemical synthesis.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Takeaway\nWater is a polar, hydrogen‑bonded molecule (H₂O) that exists as solid, liquid, and gas under Earth‑normal conditions. Its unique physical properties—high heat capacity, surface tension, density anomaly, and excellent solvent ability—make it the “universal solvent” and a cornerstone of Earth’s climate, geology, and all known life."
